    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        84.99369812011719,
        514.9307861328125,
        222.95361328125
      ],
      "text": "evacuated to three island sanctuaries: Codfish Island, Maud Island and Little\nBarrier Island. However, breeding success was hard to achieve. Rats were\nfound to be a major predator of kakapo chicks and an insufficient number of\nchicks survived to offset adult mortality. By 1995, although at least 12 chicks\nhad been produced on the islands, only three had survived. The kākāpō\npopulation had dropped to 51 birds. The critical situation prompted an urgent\nreview of kākāpō management in New Zealand.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        236.07470703125,
        519.9475708007812,
        413.75360107421875
      ],
      "text": "In 1996, a new Recovery Plan was launched, together with a specialist advisory\ngroup called the Kākāpō Scientific and Technical Advisory Committee and a\nhigher amount of funding. Renewed steps were taken to control predators on\nthe three islands. Cats were eradicated from Little Barrier Island in 1980, and\npossums were eradicated from Codfish Island by 1986. However, the\npopulation did not start to increase until rats were removed from all three\nislands, and the birds were more intensively managed. This involved moving\nthe birds between islands, supplementary feeding of adults and rescuing and\nhand-raising any failing chicks.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        426.87469482421875,
        518.8703002929688,
        505.1925964355469
      ],
      "text": "After the first five years of the Recovery Plan, the population was on target. By\n2000, five new females had been produced, and the total population had\ngrown to 62 birds. For the first time, there was cautious optimism for the\nfuture of kākāpō and by June 2020, a total of 210 birds was recorded.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        518.314697265625,
        519.62060546875,
        616.5526123046875
      ],
      "text": "Today, kākāpō management continues to be guided by the kākāpō Recovery\nPlan. Its key goals are: minimise the loss of genetic diversity in the kākāpō\npopulation, restore or maintain sufficient habitat to accommodate the\nexpected increase in the kākāpō population, and ensure stakeholders continue\nto be fully engaged in the preservation of the species.\n"
    },
